# mkdb weekly sync schedule. Source of truth — what `crontab -l` on the VPS
# should show. Install (or re-install after a rebuild) with:
#
#     crontab scripts/mkdb.crontab
#
# Verify with `crontab -l`. The VPS timezone is America/New_York, so the
# times below are Eastern (check with `timedatectl`; if it ever drifts off
# America/New_York, run `timedatectl set-timezone America/New_York` first).
#
# Why `\%F` instead of `%F`: cron treats an unescaped `%` as a newline inside
# the command field, which truncates the command. The backslash forces a
# literal `%`, so when cron actually invokes the shell, `$(date +%F)` runs
# at fire time and the log file is dated for the day it actually ran.

PATH=/usr/bin:/bin

# Sun 5:00 PM ET — fire the scheduler wrapper, which sleeps until the
# computed start time (next midnight − max of last 5 syncs' duration − 30 min
# buffer) and then runs the actual sync. 5 PM gives the wrapper up to 7
# hours of headroom — plenty for any realistic max-duration since steady-
# state syncs (metro + lank) run ~42-49 min. See scripts/scheduled-sync.ts.
0 17 * * 0 cd /root/mkdb && npm run sync:scheduled >> dumps/sync_$(date +\%F).log 2>&1

# Mon 12:00 AM ET, then hourly through Monday — the weekly promote chain,
# gated on scripts/preflight-promote.ts.
#
# Hourly rather than once because the sync now waits out a Letterboxd outage
# (re-probing every 30 min) instead of publishing a partial week. That can push
# the sync well past midnight, so a single fixed promote would simply miss the
# week. The gate makes reruns safe and cheap:
#   - this week already promoted  -> exits 1, chain stops, no output
#   - a sync is still running     -> exits 1, waits for the next tick
#   - any member incomplete       -> exits 1; emails once, 14h into the week
#   - staging complete            -> exits 0, the chain below runs
# So the first tick where the data is good promotes, and every later tick is a
# no-op. A week can no longer be lost to a slow sync.
#
# The chain itself (each step gated on the prior succeeding, so stale data
# never propagates):
#   1. promote: swap staging into live, recompute similarity, append the new
#      ranking week, trim history, delete orphan films + posters (~30 sec)
#   2. push the metro top 1000 to Letterboxd   (~7-8 min)
#   3. push the lank top 1000 to Letterboxd     (~7-8 min)
#   4. post the weekly status update to Discord #mank with 5 grid images
#      (needs DISCORD_TOKEN; a few seconds).
0  * * * 1 cd /root/mkdb && npm run preflight-promote && npm run promote && npm run update-letterboxd-list:metro && npm run update-letterboxd-list:lank && npm run post-weekly-update >> dumps/promote_$(date +\%F).log 2>&1

# Hourly — check the live site and email on failure.
#
# Added after 2026-09-20, when a Letterboxd outage mid-sync left 26 members
# with no watched count, promote swapped that into live, and the members page
# crashed client-side for ~14 hours before anyone noticed. Every layer was
# returning 200s, so the check asserts on response *content*, not status codes.
# It emails once per failure and stays quiet until the check recovers.
0  * * * * cd /root/mkdb && npm run healthcheck >> dumps/healthcheck_$(date +\%F).log 2>&1
